# stackcone Solutions posts

When the user pastes a **problem statement**, an **Upwork job post**, or asks for a **solution post**, produce a full implementation-style brief in `solutions/` — same visual system as `blog/` (charts, pipelines, tables, SEO). The page should read like a technical proposal **plus** a build guide: how to architect, implement, operate, and report.

## Deliverables (always)

1. `solutions/{slug}.html` — published page
2. `solutions/{slug}.md` — markdown source (mirror of content)
3. Update `solutions/solutions.json` — add entry with `"author": "Amar Kumar"`
4. Update `sitemap.xml` — add `<url>` for the new HTML page

**Slug:** lowercase, hyphenated (e.g. `wordpress-seo-multi-agent-pipeline`). HTML filename = `{slug}.html`.

## solutions.json entry

```json
{
  "id": "{slug}",
  "title": "Outcome-focused title",
  "description": "Problem + approach in one sentence (under ~155 chars)",
  "href": "./{slug}.html",
  "date": "YYYY-MM-DD",
  "dateLabel": "Month YYYY",
  "author": "Amar Kumar",
  "category": "RAG | Data | API | Mobile | Docs | Platform | Automation",
  "tags": ["3–6 tags"],
  "client": "Company name if mentioned — omit otherwise",
  "source": "upwork"
}
```

Omit `source` if not from Upwork. Omit `client` if no company named.

## Article structure (required)

Use `blog-page` body, `blog-article`, `../styles.css` + `../blog/blog.css` + `solutions.css`, `/site-nav.js`, `/script.js`.

| Section | ID | Content |
|---------|-----|---------|
| Title + meta | — | H1; `<p class="meta">By Amar Kumar</p>` (no date on page) |
| Summary | — | 2–3 sentences: outcome and approach |
| Table of contents | `.toc` | All major sections |
| Job context | `#job-context` | **Required for Upwork posts:** distilled brief, niche, constraints, client technical level |
| Problem | `#problem` | Pain points, why manual flow fails, YMYL/regulatory notes if relevant |
| Requirements | `#requirements` | Functional + non-functional bullets from the brief |
| Architecture | `#architecture` | ASCII diagram in `.arch-diagram` + `.pipeline` for main flow |
| Recommended stack | `#recommended-stack` | Table + **why this stack** (vs n8n-only, vs alternatives) |
| Agent / component design | `#agent-design` | Per-agent responsibilities, inputs/outputs, QA gates |
| Implementation plan | `#implementation` | `.impl-phase` blocks — **4–6 phases** with deliverables, risks, rollback |
| Reporting & ops | `#reporting` | Dashboards, APIs (GSC, Bing, rank tracking), alert cadence |
| What we built | `#what-we-built` | 5+ concrete deliverables |
| Effort estimate | `#estimate` | Hours range, hourly fit, ongoing maintenance — for Upwork-style scope |
| Charts | — | **At least 2** Chart.js charts in `.chart-wrap` |
| Glossary | `#glossary` | Terms table |
| CTA | `.blog-cta` | Upwork + Contact links |

## Upwork job posts

- Parse mandatory skills, budget band, duration, and “what they want built” into Requirements and Agent design
- Address explicit proposal asks (stack choice, reporting, examples approach) in **Recommended stack** and **Reporting**
- Do **not** fabricate client names or fake case-study traffic numbers; describe credible architecture and measurable KPIs instead
- `source: "upwork"` in JSON; optional one-line in Job context that this maps to an hourly engagement

## When a **company name** is mentioned

Set `client`, name in Problem, full component diagram, 4–6 implementation phases with timelines, ops runbooks, extra chart (deployment or request lifecycle).

## HTML conventions

- SEO: title, description, canonical, OG/Twitter, JSON-LD `Article` + `BreadcrumbList` (Home → **Solutions** → title)
- Pipelines: `.pipeline` > `.pipeline-step` + `.pipeline-arrow`
- Charts: Chart.js CDN; palette `['#2563eb', '#7c3aed', '#db2777', '#ea580c', '#16a34a', '#0891b2']`
- Visible author: **By Amar Kumar**

## Quality bar

- Teach how to build the system, not just what it does
- Problem → architecture → stack → phases → reporting must be one coherent story
- No lorem ipsum; no thin bullet lists without explanation
